<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grimoald I of Benevento</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 610–671</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Grimoald, King of the Lombards</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grimoald I was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> from 651 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a> from 662 to 671. A son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gisulf-ii-of-friuli">Gisulf II of Friuli</a>, he seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> throne during a quarrel between two royal brothers. As king he helped drive off the invasion of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> in 663, repelled Frankish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> attacks, and added new laws to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-rothari">Edict of Rothari</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grimoald was the youngest son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gisulf-ii-of-friuli">Gisulf II of Friuli</a> and the Bavarian princess Ramhilde, daughter of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garibald-i-of-bavaria">Garibald I of Bavaria</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 611, Gisulf's army was outnumbered and the duchy overrun, and Gisulf died in battle. Grimoald and his brother Radoald escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>, where they were adopted by Duke Arechis, a distant kinsman. From 641 to 642 the brothers served as regents to Arechis's son Duke Aiulf I, who was killed in 642 defending against Slavic invaders; Radoald succeeded him, and Grimoald later succeeded Radoald as Duke of Benevento. As duke he defended the Sanctuary of Monte Sant'Angelo on Mount Gargano against &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>&quot;, possibly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, who had come to plunder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 661 Aripert I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a>, was succeeded by his sons Perctarit, who governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and Godepert, from Pavia. In 662 the brothers fell out, and Godepert sent Garipald, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, to persuade Grimoald to help in a war against Perctarit. With Garibald's connivance, Grimoald assassinated Godepert and forced Perctarit to flee. He took over as king, married Godepert's sister Theodota to associate himself with the dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a>, and passed the dukedom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> to his eldest son Romuald.</p><p>He led his armies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, while Romuald took Taranto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a>. Grimoald took Forlì from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and razed Oderzo, where his brothers Tasso and Kakko had been murdered years before. When Duke Lupus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>, left as regent in the north, rebelled, Lupus was killed and his duchy destroyed with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, and Grimoald defeated Lupus's son Arnefrit and his Slavic allies at Nimis. He also defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, who invaded during the infancy of Chlothar III, and suppressed the autonomy of the duchies of Friuli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Grimoald remained nominally Arian despite his marriage to a Catholic; according to the Vita Sancti Barbati he and Romuald still practised the ancient pagan rites. He distanced himself from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and saw Saint Michael as the warrior-protector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> nation, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Wodan</a>, and had San Michele Maggiore built over the site of the palace chapel in Pavia. He died in 671 after concluding a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and was buried in the Basilica of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> in Pavia. His son Garibald was elected to succeed him but was deposed within three months by the returning Perctarit. Grimoald was remembered as generous and merciful as well as ruthless in war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grimoald, King of the Lombards” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Benevento was effectively independent of Pavia; Duke <b>Grimoald I</b> even became King of the Lombards in 662.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-benevento">Duchy of Benevento</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cividale-del-friuli">Cividale del Friuli</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Pavia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.09,13.44&amp;year=649" aria-label="Cividale del Friuli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4867 541 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Grimoald I of Benevento"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5317 811h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5317 811h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.09,13.44&amp;year=649">What was here in 649 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
